// Practice quiz: Controller, service and repository layers
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-data-jpa/controller-service-and-repository-layers

::o1 rule location::Where does the rule that a customer may hold at most two bookings belong? {
=The service#Correct. Business rules belong below the web layer so every caller shares them.
~The controller#Not quite. A job or other caller would skip it there.
~The repository#Not quite. Repositories read and write data.
~The client JSON#Not quite. A client cannot be trusted to enforce it.
}

::o1 status location::Which layer chooses the HTTP status code? {
=The controller or web layer#Correct. Status codes are HTTP concerns.
~The repository#Not quite. It knows nothing about HTTP.
~The entity#Not quite. An entity maps data.
~The database#Not quite. The database knows nothing about HTTP.
}

::o2 refactor::What is the first step when moving a rule out of a controller that calls a repository? {
=Create a service method and move the rule there#Correct. Then the controller calls that method.
~Delete the repository#Not quite. The service still needs it.
~Copy the rule into each caller#Not quite. That is the duplication you are removing.
~Add a second controller#Not quite. That does not move the rule.
}

::o3 direction::What is the correct dependency direction? {
=Controller to service to repository#Correct. Each layer calls only the one below.
~Repository to service to controller#Not quite. That points upward.
~Service to controller#Not quite. Services should not depend on the web layer.
~Repository to controller#Not quite. That skips layers upward.
}

::o3 why direction matters::Why should a service not import servlet classes? {
=So it can be called and tested without HTTP#Correct. Jobs and tests can use it directly.
~Servlet classes are deprecated#Not quite. That is not the reason.
~Services cannot import anything#Not quite. They import what they need below them.
~It would slow compilation#Not quite. That is not the concern.
}

